/* — Starlight chrome neutralisation —
   W1 rehosts the mdBook landing inside Starlight's shell, so the page now sits
   inside a document frame it was never written for. These rules strip that
   frame back to nothing, so the landing renders as the standalone, full-bleed
   document it already is. Nothing here is a design decision: WP-R rebuilds the
   landing for real and this block goes with it.

   Astro emits `is:global` styles unlayered and Starlight's own rules live in
   `@layer starlight.core`, so unlayered wins without `!important`. The one
   exception is `main`'s padding, which Starlight sets from an unlayered scoped
   rule in `Page.astro` — that one is out-specified instead. */

/* The fixed site header, and the skip link that targets the page title. */
.page > .header { display: none; }
.sl-skip-link { display: none; }

/* The landing carries its own <h1> and its own <footer>. Starlight's footer is
   a direct child of `.sl-container`; the landing's sits deeper, inside
   `.sl-markdown-content`, so the child combinators leave it alone. */
h1#_top { display: none; }
main > .content-panel > .sl-container > footer { display: none; }

/* `.main-frame`'s top padding exists only to clear the now-hidden header, and
   `scroll-padding-top` only to keep an anchor jump clear of it. With the header
   gone both are dead offsets — leaving the second one makes the "Install grim"
   button land 5rem short of the `#install` band it points at. */
.main-frame { padding-top: 0; }
html { scroll-padding-top: 0; }

/* The landing's own `.wrap` supplies the width and the gutters, and its
   `#install` band is deliberately full-bleed. */
.page .main-frame main { padding: 0; }
.content-panel { padding: 0; }
.content-panel + .content-panel { border-top: 0; }
.sl-container { max-width: none; margin-inline: 0; }
.sl-container > * + * { margin-top: 0; }
